#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aeeaf85-0cbd-58ab-91d6-93448ff54b4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Initial Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96d5056b-2381-5c59-9e5a-0dc070dcc453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,547" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326145} {:answer/id #uuid "6dfdd65d-d9ca-5787-a537-f31f96aba869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,247" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326146} {:answer/id #uuid "22717357-976d-51f0-b88e-265401ec0e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,257" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326147} {:answer/id #uuid "47770667-4e39-5080-8a3e-093761f1dd56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,542" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326148} {:answer/id #uuid "bad84617-8ac1-546f-9d83-a6691d8f8bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,542" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96d5056b-2381-5c59-9e5a-0dc070dcc453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,547" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326145} :answer-field/id #uuid "92dd5fbe-3bce-5bb4-a77f-c29eae9a960c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b39468d-5d98-5128-aa1e-9a26b817cf16" :question/math-academy-id "q-339239" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of fish in a pond after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=0.09P(t).$ If the fish population was $8\\,000$ after $18$ weeks, calculate the fish population after $23$ weeks." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.09t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial fish population.\n\nWe are given that after $18$ weeks, the population was $8\\,000.$ So, substituting $t=18$ and $P(18)=8\\,000$ into our general solution and solving for ${P}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(18) & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.09\\cdot 18} \\\\ 8\\,000 & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{1.62} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{8\\,000}{{e}^{1.62}} \\\\  & ≈1\\,583.190,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,583.190\\,{e}^{0.09t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the fish population after $23$ weeks. So, substituting $t=23$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(23) & =1\\,583.190\\,{e}^{0.09\\cdot 23} \\\\  & =1\\,583.190\\,{e}^{2.07} \\\\  & ≈12\\,547,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number. \n\nTherefore, approximately $12\\,547$ fish will be in the pond after $23$ weeks."}]}
